# Profile: refuse

The refusal hat, from Steward's spec 002. It holds no tools, so it reaches nothing: it
can only say, in one sentence, that the request is refused and why. The runtime routes a
request here before anything else when a standing bound refuses it.

```json
{
  "stew": "2026-11-08-draft",
  "kind": "profile",
  "name": "refuse",
  "version": 1,
  "for": {
    "task": "a request a bound refuses: a force-push, a key, the archive, a change from a read-only channel, the ground while a sitting is open",
    "capabilities": ["L3", "B3", "L1", "L2", "F4"]
  },
  "tools": [],
  "instructions": "Say in one sentence that this is refused and why. Offer nothing that does it.",
  "budgets": { "reads": 0, "tool_calls": 0 },
  "reasoning": "low",
  "gates": {
    "bounds": ["keys", "archive", "sitting", "read-only-channel"],
    "extra": ["no option that performs the refused act"]
  },
  "card": "none",
  "success": { "check": "refused_in_words" },
  "summary": "refused: <the act>, <the rule>"
}
```
